policy_note:
max_onset, measured over all 11,846 rows with a filled ipa column: 1 against 2 differs on 1,327 rows, 2 against 3 on 27. Vietnamese has no native consonant cluster at all, so the naive setting is 1 -- but the 2000 rules license four onsets (br, khr, xc, đr), and reader_onsets expresses exactly that licence, which max_onset alone cannot: it names those clusters in IPA and drops every other one the target's own phonotactics would have offered. So max_onset is 2 with a seventeen-entry whitelist. Word-initial onsets are unaffected by either setting, which is where the tradition's own clusters live (Brúc-xen, Xcaclati, Sếch-xpia). max_onset 3 would only add three-consonant onsets, which the licence does not cover.

The engine change that would pay for itself here: normalise the syllable to NFC *after* the syllable_fixups loop as well as before it (one line, next to the existing text = text.normalize('NFC')). A Vietnamese tone mark has to move leftwards from the end of the syllable onto the head of the rime, and a fixup can reorder characters but cannot compose them -- so the mark has to be spelled as a precomposed letter, and the rule has to be written once per vowel per tone. With a post-fixup NFC the tone rules could emit the combining mark itself and three position rules would do the whole job:
  {match: "(ch|[ptc])([\u0300\u0309\u0303])$", out: "\\1\u0323"}
  {match: "((?:ng|nh|ch|[mnptcl])+)([\u0300\u0301\u0303\u0309\u0323])$", out: "\\2\\1"}
  {match: "([aăâeêioôơuư])([iyou])([\u0300\u0301\u0303\u0309\u0323])$", out: "\\1\\3\\2"}
Fifty-five rules become three, and they stop being a table of characters and start being a statement of the rule (“the mark goes before the coda, and on the head of a falling diphthong”). Nothing else in this file needs it, and the table is complete and gap-free without it.
